{
  "note": "The read-backs of one folder attachment (ticket 26/09/30/NON-DESTRUCTIVE-HISTORY-EDITING design §22.22, live fault F4). Each case is what the attachments of one document see in order — `absent` (the bind's first read found the folder empty) or an `archive` with what the program answers to a merge (`merged` events taken and events `ahead`, or `unmergeable`: another document's archive, or one whose members differ) and to a load (`loaded`, `stale`: superseded, the program does not hold it, `cancelled`, `failed`) — and `calls` is exactly what the shell drives, in order: `merge`, `load`, `history`, `refresh` (the last two in either order, written as `history`, `refresh`), `write` (the folder's archive is written), `detach`. The first archive after an attach loads; later ones merge without replacing the document; a merge that took nothing re-reads nothing; the folder is written only when the program is ahead of it or the folder was empty. `outcomes` is how each event ends for the program: `adopted` (an empty folder took the program's document), `held` (the archive's events are in the program's document), `superseded`, `detached`, `cancelled`, `failed` or `reattached`. Nothing of the program is ever written over an archive it did not take (live fault F13 and the coordinator's decision on it): when the FIRST archive of an attachment is not loaded — the load answers `cancelled` (the person cancelled it) or `failed` — the shell is asked to `detach` the folder, which stays remembered so that it is offered again, the event ends `detached`, no failure is told for a cancel, and later events of that attachment drive nothing; `reconnect` is the person reattaching the remembered folder, a new attachment whose first archive is loaded again. Once the folder is adopted, a cancelled load of a later read-back ends `cancelled` and a failed one `failed` (the read-back rejects); the document stays attached and written. While a load runs, the shell shows its polled status outside the History panel from the first status the host polls until the load ends; a load that ends within its first poll may show none (F5).",
